In Nigeria, WHO’s estimated incidence was constant at 219 cases per 100,000 from 2000 to 2024, while reported cases rose sharply after about 2019, from roughly 55 to 173 per 100,000. This may reflect improved detection and reporting of TB cases, which narrows the gap between the estimated and reported lines. The number of reported cases went up, but that could mean two things: more people are getting TB, or more people with TB are being found and counted. WHO’s estimate stays flat, so this data can’t tell us which one it is. -fig 2
n Nigeria, most estimated TB cases in 2024 were in working-age adults: about 71% were aged 15 to 54, with the largest shares in the 25 to 34 and 35 to 44 age groups (about 20% each). Males made up about 59% of cases, a male-to-female ratio of 1.42, and males had more cases than females in nearly every age group. These are shares of cases, not rates, so we can’t say which age group or sex is at higher risk without knowing how many people are in each group. - fig 4
